"You are a helpful AI assistant that answers questions based on the provided context.\n\nTOPIC OVERVIEW:\nPOSITIVE TOPICS:\n- Parents discuss comprehensive strategies for managing children's school clothing including wardrobe planning, seasonal needs, organization methods, labeling decisions, and storage solutions.\n\nNEUTRAL TOPICS:\n- Parents discuss the challenges of finding properly fitting, affordable, and age-appropriate clothing for their growing children while balancing budget constraints, style preferences, and various occasion requirements.\n- Parents discuss managing limited finances while purchasing children's clothing and dealing with kids' awareness of economic disparities compared to their peers.\n- Parents discuss comprehensive cost tracking and practical management strategies for baby care expenses including diapers, formula, and associated childcare challenges during early development.\n- Parents discuss strategies for teaching children household responsibility through chores and room cleaning while addressing common challenges with maintaining organized living spaces.\n- Parents discuss strategies for carrying essential items and clothes when traveling or preparing for emergencies with infants and toddlers.\n- Parents discuss strategies and challenges for selecting birthday and Christmas gifts for children, particularly managing early shopping while dealing with rapidly changing interests and age-appropriate present selection.\n\nNEGATIVE TOPICS:\n- Parents discuss the overwhelming daily challenges of managing childcare routines including feeding, clothing, and caring for children while struggling to find quality time for meaningful family interactions.\n- Parents discuss children's clothing refusal behaviors, sensory sensitivities with garments, and early body image concerns that impact daily routines and activities.\n- Discussions focus on complex family dynamics involving stepmothers, foster care arrangements, and parenting challenges with troubled teenagers from unstable backgrounds.\n- Parents discuss strategies for managing overwhelming daily laundry loads and share techniques for reducing the frequency of washing clothes while maintaining household cleanliness standards.\n- Parents discuss strategies and challenges related to managing morning routines and getting children ready for school or daycare on time.\n- Parents discuss managing children's behavioral challenges involving anger outbursts, clothing resistance, and throwing behaviors during daily routines and school transitions.\n- Parents discuss ongoing struggles with children, particularly adolescent boys, who resist basic hygiene practices like showering, using deodorant, and wearing clean clothes despite persistent parental efforts.\n- Parents discuss challenging child behaviors including excessive talking, meanness toward family members, defiant attitudes, and difficulty listening or following instructions at home.\n\n    \nCONTEXT:\n[{'id': '1nm0nzs', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1nm0nzs: Where to buy clothes for my tiny goth?\\n\\nShe\\'s 9 and has complained for years that the clothes in the kid\\'s section are \"too happy.\" I tried boy\\'s clothes, those are apparently also not what she\\'s looking for. To anyone suggesting plain solid colors, she\\'ll tolerate them but finds them boring.\\nShe loves band shirts (though her tastes are very metal), flames, dragons, spikes, basically her room looks like Bowser\\'s lair. Problem is, she\\'s not even close to adult sizes (she\\'s in a size 8 for an oversized fit), and I don\\'t know where to find goth girl/metalhead couture in kids medium.\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment nfa1y2y: Hot topic???? They mostly cater to junior sizes and def run small so that would be my first recommendation (especially for band materials etc)\\n    - Comment nfab7b7: this! their stuff is so small. theyre a little less emo/goth nowadays and more pop culture, but she\\'ll definitely find some items.\\n\\nbut be prepared to fight tooth and nail over giving them your email address to sign up for hot cash 🙄 almost left in the middle of my last in-store purchase because they WOULD NOT QUIT.\\n    - Comment nfaf02y: I didn\\'t know hot topic had smaller sizes, we\\'ll definitely look there.\\n    - Comment nfax1us: My 8 and 10 year olds have been eyeing up the Metallica tees there.'}, {'id': '1njwjns', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1njwjns: Matching clothes for siblings\\n\\nDoes anyone know of children’s clothing brands that make matching clothes for siblings of different ages and genders? Wanting to match my kids but don’t know where to start! \\n\\nEdit: we don’t celebrate Christmas so looking for non-Christmas stuff\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment netjj5c: Gymboree\\nChildren’s Place'}, {'id': '1mv9bbv', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1mv9bbv: Weird and funny fashion in clothing that our kids wear.\\n\\nThe weird change of fashion. My kids have some funny understanding about clothing that i would never do but its totally ok for them. \\n\\n1. Wearing different socks. Its totally okey for them to wear different coloured socks every day. I have tried to do that myself after them, but looks too weird for me. \\n2. Wearing flipflops and socks. When i was young its was the biggest turnaway to look like this but mine and all my neighbour kids do it all the time. \\n3. My 8 year old kid went to national theatre and i gave him festive white shirt and long tie to wear but when i looked the pictures aferwards he had put the orange school sweatshirt on to it and the long tie was on top. And even teacher didn't care to say to him that it looks weird. \\n\\nHave you noticed something similar or is it just me?\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment n9ol7o6: I wouldn’t call it fashion by my six yr old constantly puts his T-shirts on backwards and when I point it out he just shrugs and says it’s fine, so I leave him to it - he has clothes on so it’s all good. He also pulls his socks up to his calves with his shorts, looks terrible to me but again he’s wearing the socks so I’m not complaining.\\n    - Comment n9ow2sp: Mine is 9 and still does this all the time (not on purpose)…..he’s always surprised I can tell…..uh, dude……the back is higher.\\n    - Comment n9pw92h: My daughter insists on wearing leggings with knee high socks that she pulls all the way up. It looks ridiculous and it can't be comfy\\n    - Comment n9qcgkx: My 5 year old does the sock thing - pulls them all the way up to just below his knees. Totally copies my FIL in his diabetics socks :D They look so hilariously adorable when together.\\n    - Comment n9w84b6: My nine year old does this too. Shirts inside out and backward. Shorts and pants backward (somehow the discomfort doesn't bother her?) and mismatched socks of different lengths. Makes me grateful for school uniforms.\"}, {'id': '1mu6x14', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1mu6x14: What are 6th grade boys wearing?\\n\\nMy youngest boy is getting ready to go to 6th grade and I’m wondering what other working-class kiddos are wearing?  For reference, we live in the PNW. He doesn’t really care about clothes yet but I don’t want him to get made fun of for wearing something wrong.  At the same time, we aren’t made of money and can’t buy expensive things.  Anyone have any advice?\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment n9gygwf: Plain Tshirts, Henleys, jeans, sweats. Hoodies. Nothing with graphics id say to start. Basics.\\n    - Comment n9hd386: You're telling a sixth grader to Don Henleys? You are definitely setting his parents up for a lot of [dirty laundry](\"}, {'id': '1mg89p2', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1mg89p2: Where to buy clothes?\\n\\nSo we have been buying the target brand Cat & Jack clothes forever now for our 9 year old boy. They always had great tshirt designs, super affordable and liked the way the tshirts felt.\\n\\nBut lately two things have happened with Target. One their tshirt colors and designs have been really bad. Like Beige and dull colors, come on its clothes for kids. And then I feel their quality has gone down too. The feel of the tshirt feels thinner / lighter.\\n\\nThere used to be a lot of options in store and online. Like all the skinny / slim stuff has disappeared from Target now, and that was the one place previously where we could get pants for our skinny guy.\\n\\nI dont like the way the Old Navy tshirts feel, they feel so rough like one wash would make them unwearable.\\n\\nWhere else can we buy clothes for boys in that same price range that feel and look good?\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment n6obg9t: Children's place has great prices and perfect pants for my skinny kids.   I actually found the slim pants were too slim, so I buy the regular.  The only downside is you have to pay to return stuff by mail.\"}, {'id': '1mbp5qv', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1mbp5qv: “Cool” Clothes?\\n\\nWhere are we shopping for cool/trendy clothes that won’t cost half my paycheck? Especially for kids that are in bigger sizes than their peers! My oldest SS is 9 and in men’s sizes looking to get some clothes for back to school and I’m at a loss! I’ve been hitting the thrift stores looking for Nike/Underarmour/Adidas, all the athletic wear brands. I’ve gotten him a couple character shirts (mainly fortnite as that’s what he likes) but it’s tough to find anything in men’s for a kid that young! What brands are cool now? Please help, I feel so out of touch!\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment n5nvi6z: This is a great age to guide your children to pick out their own clothing. They are starting to look around and know what other kids are wearing. \\n\\nGoing to school? Mostly a free for all, with some exceptions (most schools have some restrictions).  Let them pick out what they love. Ask questions about items you think might look good. You might get a response on why that item isn’t as cool as you think it is. \\n\\nGoing to Church/dinner out at a restaurant?  Explain why everyone needs a go-to button up, polo, and chino pants. Proper clothes for the proper occasion. \\n\\nSet a budget and see if they can stick to it.  Give them the cash and a list of items they need (3 shirts, 2 shorts, jeans). At this age, they are growing quickly, so it’s important to have them try on their clothing in store. They are also at the age where they probably don’t want you in the dressing room with them so let them have their space. \\n\\nWe usually find a good selection at Gap and Old Navy, and shop their sales heavily.  Our kids are younger, but we frequently let them pick out one shirt/dress if they are with us. \\n\\nAnd finally, although you retain veto privileges, try to be flexible.  I have a visceral disdain for Crocs yet my son really wanted a pair. I allowed it and they are now his go-to pair. Turns out his best friend had a pair and now they match at summer camp.\\n    - Comment n5nwzqb: My step son would rather play video games than go shopping with me lol and we only see them every other weekend so we try to do fun stuff! I do plan on having him try everything on before we send it home to his mom’s and making sure he likes what I ordered and will return anything he doesn’t like!\\xa0\\n\\nI also love the old navy sales and go there often to get clothes for all 3 kids!\\xa0\\n\\nThank you for the advice :)\\n    - Comment n5obdy7: Yup. Don't micromanage things like color, my dad did this and to this day I don't understand why he cared so much lol. Huge power struggles if you wanted a color that wasn't primary. \\n\\nSee what they find within the budget; it might mean they splurge on one item you think is silly, and learn that it means going thriftier on the rest of the items, and be A-OK with that trade-off!\"}, {'id': '1mao3uk', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1mao3uk: 5 year old won’t wear clothes (going on 6 days in a row )\\n\\nWe have a 5 year old and 2.5 year old. As of 6 days ago the 5 year old has stopped wearing clothes - yesterday she has also stopped wearing underwear. \\n\\nShe says that anything she wears is “too tight” and has a major meltdown. We are at the ends of our sanity. By major meltdown we are talking an hour+ tantrum, running screaming hitting things. She will strip off all articles of clothing. \\n\\nShe indicates that she does really want to go places and looks forward to it but cannot try any clothes on without shifting into a tantrum. \\n\\nShe does have a minor history with sensory issues and has called things “too tight” before but this has usually been a preference indicator where she will choose another article for clothing. She is otherwise social and intelligent, and hasn’t ever done this before. She attended preschool. \\n\\nShe says that she is “figuring it out” and that she needs “many days” to figure out clothes. She often talks about the fun things she will do when she is ready for clothes again. Her appetite, diet, sleep, and everything else behavior wise is completely normal. \\n\\nWe have tried \\n-reinforcing the need for wearing clothes (we can’t go anywhere since people need clothes for that)\\n\\n-incentivizing special circumstances (if we put our clothes on we can go to a toy store or to grandmas house)\\n\\n-changing the situation (getting dressed outside or in the car, mid-action) \\n\\n-having everyone else get dressed together \\n\\n-play dress up games and be silly about dressing with little pressure \\n\\n-making being around the house very boring and unengaging, up to and including not doing things because those things are for people with clothes (she waits it out) \\n\\n-having clothed friends (largely cousins) over for social situations \\n\\n-taking a few day break where we don’t talk about clothes or getting dressed at all\\n\\n-introducing non-clothing ways for her body to feel pressure and texture (brushing, blankets, etc as a part of other activities or while she is distracted)\\n\\n-slipping a dress on her at night while she is sleeping\\n\\n-introducing this as a conversation with her and asking for her advice on what we can do to help her\\n\\n-frankly, in the first couple of days, attempting to get a dress on and get her into the car (massive tantrum) \\n\\nOver the last few days, we \\n-talked to her occupational therapist \\n\\n-talked to a occupational therapist specializing in sensory issues \\n\\n-talked to an emergency pediatric psychologist \\n\\nIt seems that what amounts to clinical or therapist consensus is to largely take all pressure to get clothes on away and encourage pressure/touch therapy and play with clothes. They suggest that hopefully this warms her up and that she has some sort of anxiety driving this. The clothing specialist said to take all pressure off for 3-5 days, keep her activities and routine as normal as possible  and see what happens. \\n\\nBeing stuck not being able to go anywhere and feeling completely defeated. I don’t know how we keep a routine normal without going anywhere. Her and the 2.5 butt heads throughout the day. We are feeling like we are stuck in a hellish time loop. Our mental health is taking a nosedive. We do try to limit the amount of exposure our daughters have to this wearing on us (taking breaks, taking turns) but that also has it limits over a week. \\n\\nDoes anyone have any experience with something like this or anything else to try that isn’t “wait and see” - end of wits and trying to stay supportive as I have to go back to work\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment n5glqrt: Hey, first of all, you’re doing a great job. You’re doing a really, really hard job, and you’re doing well. You’re talking to professionals and to your child and to your partner, and your daughter is going to make it out of this phase okay.\\n\\nThe only suggestion I really have for you is to maybe get her a soft robe to wear around the house. Fleecy, if she likes that texture, or satin or cotton- whatever texture she likes in a blanket/ sheets. I also googled “autism underwear” to find some sensory-friendly underwear, and I found some boy short style underwear that might feel better to her. Maybe her undies ride up, and that bothers her… no idea.\\n\\nYou’ve got this! Keep taking breaks, and keep looking for answers.'}, {'id': '1m9mh4e', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1m9mh4e: Struggling with my 3 year old girl and clothes\\n\\nI have a 3 year old daughter and she goes to kindergarten, i have a daily struggle with “clothes” and every single morning she goes on a tantrum about it, she refuses to wear anything and just cries hysterically for about 30 minutes before i force the uniform on her\\nNot only when it comes to her kindergarten clothes but also whenever she wants to wear anything or change whatever she is wearing.. its just too much to do this everyday\\nOn the other hand i have a son who does not mind if i put a bag of potato on him as a jacket.. \\nso i dont know how to deal with her for this, any help ?\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment n58f6ra: I have a friend who has her daughter literally sleep in her clothes for the next day so there’s not changing at all in the morning. 🤷\\u200d♀️\\n    - Comment n5a1dey: Yup my daughter sleeps in her princess dresses and wears them to school the next day. Just take off the night time pull up and throw on a pair of shorts and she’s ready to go.'}, {'id': '1m8rnxe', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1m8rnxe: School clothing style\\n\\nWhen you are purchasing your child’s school clothes do you keep a theme in mind? Or do you just buy whatever you find?\\n\\nMy son will hopefully start preschool on August so I want to take advantage of the sales and buy his school clothes, but I don’t know how to dress him. He’s always been home and when we have appointments he gets dressed in the nice clothes. But if he’s just at home he’s in comfy clothes like jersey pants and short sleeve shirts. So would it be better for him to be sent to school in comfortable or in presentable clothing?\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment n52nfjt: I always kept it very basic for school and sort of created our own uniform. I have lived in both warm climates (mostly warm all year) and colder climates (traditional heavy, freezing winter w/ snow & ice). \\n\\nFor the warmer seasons: Basic shorts in blue, khaki, and brown or grey (b/c black is too hot). I try to have 6-8 pairs of shorts. That way we can get through a week of school without having to wash and then have at least 1 extra to send to school for an emergency, with potentially a few extra pairs in case I forgot to do laundry or something happens and you just run through everything. If you see a mostly clean pair one day, I\\'m happy to \"air and wear\" after a day, but for most of elementary school this isn\\'t an option at all.  Then for shirts I do about 8-10 [short sleeved] b/c they tend to gather stains and rips faster. I try to make sure I choose colors that will go with any of the shorts or at least half of the shorts. I also will generally buy at least 1 white t-shirt (b/c they always end up doing an event at some point and ask kids to wear a white t-shirt). And I buy 2 school shirts. One for spirit day, one for pictures or fieldtrips. I do keep a stain stick by the front door so when kids come in from school I can stain-stick them immediately and toss shirts into the laundry. [Also I make my kids change out of school clothes after getting home so they don\\'t get dinner on them or get them messy during after-school playtime.]\\n\\nFor winter, I grab about 6 long-sleeve t-shirts to put under regular t-shirts to keep kids warmer. And for even our warmer climate this is all we use most of the winter to keep warm. I have girls so my go-to was leggings and sweat pants b/c leggings are cheaper than jeans. I\\'d put the leggings on & they could cover with sweat pants. For boys I assume you could still choose sweat pants but also use jeans. In my warmer climate I only buy about 4 pairs of leggings w/ 2 pairs of sweat pants but in colder climates I used 6-8 pairs of leggings and 6 sweats. At the time the hanes matching tops & bottoms - I would actually buy several sets for school and play. \\n\\nI buy my kids 2-3 hoodies for cooler weather now & air them out between uses, but still wash every week/every other week. They also all have 1-2 flannel shirts. In the colder climate I had 2 \"nice\" winter coats - one to wear, one to air, so that if they came home damp I could let it dry out a full day. Also a fan of the snow bibs, so I usually had a few pairs of those b/c they went outside even when it had snowed. \\n\\nTwo pairs of regular shoes - so if they got wet or yucky they could be cleaned off & left to air while they wore another pair. And 1 pair of snow boots - and I did buy a size up most years so they could use them all winter, and I did buy a NICE pair so they stayed dry on the inside. \\n\\nI always kept it basic. Shorts, leggings/sweatpants or jeans, t-shirts, hanes sweatshirts.\\n    - Comment n52wg4v: Yes! I also wanted to create a uniform of his own. To me that’s a t-shirt and jeans. We live in a snow free area so it usually just rains or gets very cold in the winter. Your quantities are so helpful and smart.'}, {'id': '1m85d9q', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1m85d9q: Just venting about gender specific character clothing\\n\\nMy 5 year old girl loves Sonic and Spiderman. When I try looking for swimsuits for her, they are nowhere to be found - for Spider-Man theme, it’s all ghost spider. For sonic, I barely found any. Even for paw patrol, it’s mostly Skye (my girl likes Chase). I’m just venting about it because I just want fun character clothing for my girl!\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment n4wk83j: Ugh, do not even get me started!! My sweet boy loves rainbows. Like, if you ask him what his favorite color is, he says rainbow. When he was about 4, Children\\'s Place had these jammies, with construction equipment and rainbows that said \"I dig rainbows\"\\n it couldn\\'t have been more perfect. I wish more companies would do more of that.\\n    - Comment n4wuuu4: You must have my son\\'s clone. Does yours also have \"tie dye\" as a close second favorite color?\\n    - Comment n4xge0j: Princess awesome makes boys’ clothes like this. They’re expensive but the prints are super cute.\\n    - Comment n4xx7t0: My son is similar age and also loves rainbows. Primary had a lot of gender neutral rainbow stuff that we have enjoyed.\\n    - Comment n504j9b: I’m your kid all grown up I love rainbows. I must recommend the ‘solar powered rainbow maker’ by kikkerland. I have two. Has a prism in it, stick it to the window and when the sun hits it it’s rainbow time. I refer to it as the rainbow hour. Cats also love it.'}, {'id': '1m8fei6', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1m8fei6: quality clothing for kids\\n\\nmini rant/ question: i have 2 kids one is almost a year and the other is 3. i’m finding it difficult to get clothes for them that are age appropriate but super fruity and cheaply made. i’m also not trying to spend 100$ a piece given the fact they grow out of their clothes faster than i can even replace them. i recently ordered some pieces from zara because the style is right up my alley, and the price wasn’t terrible. HOWEVER, i’m a bit confused as to why they’re allowed to charge 32$ a piece when their clothes look and feel like a bedsheet i picked up from walmart for 99¢?! i’m extremely disappointed in the quality of the clothing for the price i paid, and am pretty bummed because thinness and horrible texture aside, the clothes are super cute and i was really looking forward to seeing the kids in them. \\n\\ni’m on the hunt for better quality clothing for kids that aren’t an arm and a leg and aren’t just unicorns and ice cream prints. if you have any stores, boutiques, or small businesses that you can recommend i’d love to try them out please and thank you!\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment n4yxyd9: Gap sales are the way!!\\n    - Comment n50lklv: Gap Factory too - the quality is honestly the same and they always have good sales'}, {'id': '1lygm5w', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1lygm5w: Where can I find fun clothes for boys?\\n\\nTwo boys moms and my boys love colors. Rainbow, purple, pink, etc and we certainly don’t discourage it. We let them like what they like. But it is so difficult to find fun clothes for boys that I often find myself also browsing the girls section. The sizes and fit are not always great coming from the girls section.\\n\\nAnyone else have my problem? Any recommendations on where to shop for boys that doesn’t only want to wear blue and green???\\n\\nThanks in advance!!\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment n2tqjfr: As a 23 year old male…. It doesnt get much better unless you have money for designer and name brands it seems.'}, {'id': '1m0tz1b', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1m0tz1b: Whoa... senior photo time already!??!? (allow me to vent and pick your collective brains)\\n\\nSenior (yearbook) photos are asking for \"Black Own Clothing\" for the photo shoot. The reference photos they sent have the boys in a black suit and tie. (Girls are in nice formal dresses)\\n\\nSo we went to two different stores to buy him a suit that he will probably only wear once in his life for a 6-minute photo session. He\\'s not gonna go to senior prom (trust me on this). If he ever has a job interview for an internship or something in college, he would be overdressed for his field of study. If we went to a family wedding (not looking likely for several years), he would wear our cultural dress. By the time he\\'s in the age range to need a suit, he will hopefully have filled out a bit.  \\n  \\nHe\\'s skinny. Like, in the 1st percentile for his height skinny. The closest size dress pants that would fit him would be 28X32, and would still need to be taken in. They happened to have a size 35 jacket that would not need to be altered, but it was around $200!\\n\\nHis entire wardrobe consists of plain t-shirts and casual pants. Why pretend he\\'s the type of kid who owns a suit? (The sales guy at Men\\'s Warehouse was amazed we were buying a suit just for a yearbook photo).\\n\\nI\\'m thinking about having him wear \"business casual\" with a black dress shirt and pants and a contrasting tie. Would he look completely out of place? I\\'m guessing it would be easier to find clothing that actually fits and he could wear again.\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment n3c58fk: Never heard of a school dictating the outfit of students for their picture. Why can\\'t he wear what he already owns or wants to wear? What are they gonna do?\\n    - Comment n3cb4xa: It\\'s not the school it\\'s the photo studio that does the yearbook pics. They give options for multiple poses: casual clothes with props, cap-and-gown, and the official yearbook photo, which is \"Black Own Clothing\". He doesn\\'t want to do the casual or cap-and-gown options.\\n\\nHe\\'s allowed to wear whatever he wants, I suppose, as long as it\\'s black. The example photos for the yearbook pics just threw me because they were all in formalwear. Maybe he could just do a Steve Jobs and wear a black turtleneck and black jeans or something?'}, {'id': '1lfrekx', 'markdown_content': \"# Post 1lfrekx: Hoodies in the middle of summer\\n\\nAnyone else have teen boys who insist on wearing hoodies and joggers when it's 90+ degrees outside? \\n\\nNote: This doesn't have to do with covering up self-harm or anything else dangerous. My foster son is fine with t-shirts and shorts in the house but outside he insists on wearing a hoodie and joggers to show off the brand. I took him to a Juneteenth festival today, 93 degrees, he insisted on wearing this. Meanwhile I'm in shorts and a lightweight top and am still sweating. Saw several boys there also in hoodies and joggers. In fact I saw more boys in hoodies than actual summer clothes. I'm also a teacher and every year the amount of boys insisting on wearing this seems to increase. I'm just wondering, is this just a thing with boys in my area or is it a thing parents in other area experience with their boys?\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment myqhanz: The same boys will wear shorts when it’s 20 degrees here.\"}, {'id': '1ld526m', 'markdown_content': '# Post 1ld526m: What\\'s \"cool\" right now in middle school?\\n\\nI have a 10 year old daughter who is entering middle school in the fall. She\\'s not nervous overall, per se, but has expressed some nervousness about not knowing what kind of clothes are trendy. We\\'ve of course talked about wearing what she\\'s comfortable in, owning her style, etc etc. But I wondered if I could also get her a little boost of confidence by getting her a few birthday gifts in August that make her feel like she\\'s up on the trends. Any insight?\\n\\n## Comments:\\n\\n- Comment my5p21p: this just popped on my reddit fyp not a parent but as a 17 year old girl in Oregon \\xa0 majority of whats \"trendy\" is the same things that were trendy for millennials think 90s early to mid 2000s fashion\\n    - Comment my5pdfn: Oh, perfect, I lived it, I\\'m a pro 😂\\n    - Comment my7ixow: I\\'ve seen so many teens who look like they fell out of a Delia\\'s catalogue\\n    - Comment my881i6: Yes! I work in an elementary school and I always think \"Man, I would\\'ve worn that in 4th grade too.\"\\nI think jeans that have really unique aspects to them, like sequins, gems, painted flowers, ripped, etc. If you\\'re crafty, you could even try to embroider or paint flowers on a pair of jeans\\n    - Comment my66i3s: Thank you!!!'}]\n\nUSER QUERY:\nwhat are the themes in clothing related to color\n\nINSTRUCTIONS:\n1. Answer the user's query based ONLY on the information provided in the context.\n2. If the user's query is not related to the context, say \"I don't have enough information to answer this question.\"\n3. If the context doesn't contain enough information to answer the query, say \"I don't have enough information to answer this question.\"\n4. Do not make up information or use knowledge outside of the provided context.\n5. For every piece of evidence you cite, add a reference at the end of the sentence in the format [postID] where postID is the exact ID of the source document.\n6. Example of proper reference: \"Users are experiencing battery life issues with the device [qpmkvr].\"\n7. Make sure all postID references are accurate and correspond to actual post IDs from the provided context.\n8. Do not modify the postID format - keep it exactly as shown in the context (do not add extra characters or modify the ID).\n9. Organize your response using markdown section headers (##) to categorize different perspectives.\n10. Use headers like \"## Positive Perspectives\", \"## Criticisms\", \"## Feature Requests\", \"## Common Issues\", etc. as appropriate for the query.\n11. Under each section header, provide a bulleted list explaining the key points with references.\n\n\nANSWER:\n"